00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:13.180
even though I love to
talk and always talk,

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:15.897
that consumption pattern has
been a very big deal for me.

00:14:15.897 --> 00:14:17.310
- So, there's a wonderful story

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:18.143
about listening.
- Please, okay.

00:14:18.143 --> 00:14:19.507
- Because the problem when people say,

00:14:19.507 --> 00:14:20.430
"You need to be a better listener,"

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.420
is we are human beings,
and we need to communicate.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:24.801
And communication is two
ways, listening and speaking.

00:14:24.801 --> 00:14:27.360
So, but everybody's like, "You
gotta be a better listener."

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.020
But here's the best
understanding I have of that.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:31.950
So, Nelson Mandela is universally regarded

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:33.140
as a great leader, which is important,

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:34.940
because different people are viewed

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:36.180
differently in different nations.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.280
But Nelson Mandela, universally regarded

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:39.470
as a great leader, right?

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:41.610
He was actually the son of a tribal chief,

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.977
and he was asked in an interview once,

00:14:43.977 --> 00:14:46.250
"How did you learn to be a great leader?"

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:47.660
And he tells the story of how he would go

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.250
to tribal meetings with his father,

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:50.900
and he remembers two things.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.040
They always sat in a
circle, and his father

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:54.330
was always the last to speak.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.684
- Hm.
- And in terms of leadership

00:14:56.684 --> 00:14:59.117
and listening, I think the idea of

00:14:59.117 --> 00:15:01.650
"be a better listener" is
actually half the advice.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:04.670
I think the advice is, practice
being the last to speak.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:05.820
You see this all the time in meetings,

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.140
where everybody will sit around a room,

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:08.647
the senior guy will be like,
"All right, here's the problem,

00:15:08.647 --> 00:15:09.480
"here's what I think we should do,

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:11.000
"but I'm really interested
in what your thoughts are."

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:11.940
- Yes.
- "Let's go around the room."

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:12.910
It's too late!

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:14.007
You've influenced the room.
- You've created

00:15:14.007 --> 00:15:16.310
the footprint.
- And people bend and mold,

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:17.470
as opposed to saying, "Here's the problem,

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:19.530
"I'm interested in what you have to say,"

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:20.363
without saying anything.

00:15:20.363 --> 00:15:23.330
And having this, and this takes practice,

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:26.950
not even giving a hint
whether you agree or disagree.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.310
If anything, you ask
questions to learn more.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:31.130
You get the benefit of
hearing everybody's opinion,

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:32.410
everybody gets to feel heard,

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:33.850
and then you get to render

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:35.144
your opinion.
- So I would tell you,

00:15:35.144 --> 00:15:37.050
and this is for people that
are running businesses,

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:40.220
that is a micro example of the way,

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:43.290
and I think makes a ton of sense.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:46.357
I would tell you, "Andy,
you obviously direct

00:15:46.357 --> 00:15:48.987
"report to me, you run our team.

00:15:48.987 --> 00:15:50.327
"I think people would be stunned

00:15:50.327 --> 00:15:52.497
"by how little we talk at all."

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:56.020
Like, the level of, micro, right?

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.650
Like, the level of
micromanagement I put on,

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:02.110
my version of that is actually
letting people do their thing

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:04.536
and watching it from, speaking last.

00:16:04.536 --> 00:16:07.890
I guess my punchline is, by
the time I get into the meeting

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:09.339
where we're like, "Here's the problem,"

00:16:09.339 --> 00:16:11.510
the amount of listening
that has been done,

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.890
because I've created such a white canvas

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.570
for the leaders to do their
thing, and I can watch it

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:19.860
and contextualize what they're
doing, is the macro version,

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:21.610
because once you're in that meeting room,

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:26.030
that's basically the final pitch of what's

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:27.980
been going on over that period of time.

00:16:29.127 --> 00:16:29.960
- Okay.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:31.890
- Yeah, it's interesting,
it's interesting.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.290
I believe in that quite a bit.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:34.830
Okay, good.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.610
I mean, I think I, on the other hand,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:40.700
do think that all skills have a max-out.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:45.190
At some level, your hard-wiring limits--

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:47.860
- So you can't continue to
grow till the day you die?

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:51.010
